<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!--
    Phase 1488 — the repo's Fable LAW harness.

    Until this landed the only Fable target in the build was `Catalog`, a transpile-only pass over
    a sample. A transpile proves the client tier COMPILES; it asserts nothing, so a claim about
    behaviour under Fable had nowhere to live and the browser merge — `TreeMerge.merge3Way`, which
    ships in `Fuaran.UI.OpStream.Dag.Merge` and runs in a browser — was demonstrated by example on
    .NET and certified nowhere.

    This project is the assertion side. It compiles on BOTH pipelines and prints the same
    fixed-shape lines on each, so `fable-check.ps1` beside it can run it twice and byte-compare:
    every line carries COUNTS, so two pipelines that are each internally lawful but disagree about
    a merge outcome still differ line-for-line. A law-only probe would report that as two greens.

    It is in `Fuaran.sln` and in `test-suites.json`, unlike its two neighbours here
    (`pure-tier-laws`, `ids-parity-probe`). That is deliberate and it is the difference between
    this project and those: the .NET leg is an ordinary Expecto-shaped console the gate already
    knows how to run, so registering it costs the gate nothing and gains it the same laws on the
    canonical pipeline. The FABLE leg needs Node and is reached through the `FableCheck` target.

    NOTHING HERE MAY USE A CONSTRUCT FABLE CANNOT LOWER. No Expecto (it does not transpile), no
    `System.IO`, no reflection, no `StringComparer`. The whole file set is compiled to JavaScript,
    which is the point.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <!--
      NULLNESS OFF, and it is load-bearing rather than laziness. Under Fable the ENTRY project's
      MSBuild properties govern the WHOLE transpiled source graph (the reason CI's
      `fable-portability` job exists at all). With nullness ON, `Fuaran.Core.Wire`'s canonical
      encoder emits `StringBuilder__ToString(sb)`, which `fable-library-js` 5.0.0 does not export
      — the transpile is GREEN and every module that imports the wire types then dies at import
      with a `SyntaxError`. `samples/apply-demo`, the only other Fable entry project in this repo
      that is actually EXECUTED, carries the same setting for the same reason.

      Nothing is lost by it: this project is the RUN side of the gate. The nullness-ON compile is
      the other half, and `fable-check.ps1` does it explicitly over the three client-tier projects
      under THEIR own settings — which is the compile CI performs and the one this repo's gate
      lacked. See `CLAUDE.md`, "Fable method traps".
    -->
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="TestSupport.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Laws.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Fuaran.UI\Fuaran.UI.fsproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Fuaran.UI.Ops\Fuaran.UI.Ops.fsproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Fuaran.UI.OpStream.Abstractions\Fuaran.UI.OpStream.Abstractions.fsproj" />
    <!-- The subject of the first law: the three-way merge that runs in the browser. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Fuaran.UI.OpStream.Dag.Merge\Fuaran.UI.OpStream.Dag.Merge.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="FSharp.Core" />
    <!-- For `emitJsStatement` alone — Fable drops `main`'s return value, so the Node exit status
         has to be set by hand. See `Program.setExitCode`. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Fable.Core" />
    <!-- The conformance kit ships `fable/` sources, so its law families transpile with the rest of
         the graph — which is what makes a Fable PORT of a family possible at all. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Fuaran.Core.Conformance" />
    <PackageReference Include="Fuaran.Core.Ops" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
